  "title": "DataAccessV1dot8",
  "description": "K-05 v1.8, a minor version of the data-access calls (T-1102, from T-1099's handoff; DL-130, renumbered by DL-344, DL-364, DL-391, DL-438 and DL-450). It only adds to v1.7 (data-access.v1.7.schema.json, frozen and unchanged, referred to here, which refers on to v1.6, v1.5, v1.4, v1.3, v1.2, v1.1 and v1): every v1.7 definition is here under its own name, the unchanged ones by reference; the changed ones, sendState.response and sendState.exchange, take two new optional fields in the answer's textsConsent, so every v1 to v1.7 exchange is a v1.8 exchange. Added: sendState's texts consent row says who gave the yes and for which booking (README item 47; RULES 7.1, 7.5; S13-32, MASTER-FLOW-A lines 7214, 7215 and 7242): fromContact, true only when the row is a yes the person sent by text from the contact it covers (K-29 v1 consents.channel text, written by a turn of that same handle: consents.turn_id and K-04 v1 turns.handle_id; or, with no turn, a START, recorded on return from that number's own text: business_id null, which K-29 v1 allows only on a STOP's or a START's row, and K-09 v1's START rule), false for every other row (a yes the business entered, a page's, an outside AI's or an email's, a yes by text from another handle, a row that withdraws); and bookingId, K-29 v1 consents.booking_id as stored (the booking a fresh yes covers; null for START's yes for every business). Both are read in sendState's own transaction from K-29 v1 consents and K-04 v1 turns, with the grants rosie_job holds (0012, 0013): K-29 v1's row holds both facts, so no K-29 minor version, column, grant, index or migration is added. A v1.8 call always writes them: sendState.v1dot8Response is its answer with both required. The stopped flag stays the phone's (K-04 phones.stopped), never derived from a consent row. Not model-facing, so number and length limits are schema rules here. Owner: the architect.",
